Docs.remarks:
## Remarks

An instance of this type is passed to the callback supplied to <xref:SkiaSharp.SKImage.RequestReadPixels*> or <xref:SkiaSharp.SKSurface.RequestReadPixels*>. It exposes the requested pixels as one or more planes; a plain (non-YUV) read produces a single interleaved plane, so <xref:SkiaSharp.SKImageReadPixelsResult.PlaneCount> is `1`.

The result and its plane data are only valid while the callback is executing. When the source is GPU-backed, the underlying data is invalidated immediately if the rendering context is abandoned or destroyed. To keep pixels beyond the callback, copy them out with <xref:SkiaSharp.SKImageReadPixelsResult.CopyPlaneTo*> or <xref:SkiaSharp.SKImageReadPixelsResult.ToArray*>, or materialize an owned object with <xref:SkiaSharp.SKImageReadPixelsResult.ToImage*> or <xref:SkiaSharp.SKImageReadPixelsResult.ToBitmap*>.

You do not create this object yourself and normally do not need to dispose it; the request infrastructure disposes it after the callback returns. Accessing any member after disposal throws <xref:System.ObjectDisposedException>.

## Examples

The following example reads back a rescaled region and materializes an owned bitmap that outlives the callback:

```csharp
using var source = SKImage.FromBitmap(new SKBitmap(200, 200));
var info = new SKImageInfo(100, 100, SKColorType.Rgba8888, SKAlphaType.Premul);
var srcRect = new SKRectI(0, 0, 200, 200);

SKBitmap result = null;
source.RequestReadPixels(info, srcRect, r => {
    if (r != null && r.PlaneCount == 1)
        result = r.ToBitmap();
});
```
